
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I put a design together: identity. The task layer needs a [38;5;153musers[39m table the moment it has an
  assignee, so this can't be deferred — and it's coupled to notifications in a way that's easy to miss.

  [1mThe coupling:[0m if notifications ever go out by email, they need a [3mverified[0m email address. Which auth mechanism you pick
  decides whether you get verification for free or have to build it yourself later.

  - [1mEmail + password, server sessions[0m — self-contained, no third-party dependency, works offline in dev. But you own
    password hashing, reset flows, and email verification — and verification is exactly the piece notifications will
    need.
  - [1mMagic link (email code/link, no password)[0m — every login [3mis[0m an email-ownership proof, so verified addresses come for
    free and there are no passwords to store or reset. Requires a working mail sender from day one, which you'll need
    for notifications regardless.
  - [1mOAuth (Google/GitHub)[0m — no credential handling at all and verified emails from the provider. Costs an external
    dependency and app registration, and complicates local dev.
  - [1mStubbed users, no real auth[0m — a dev-only switch that seeds a few users. Fastest to a demo; must be replaced before
    anything real, and "replace auth later" reliably costs more than it saves.

  I'd recommend [1mmagic link[0m. It's the only option where the infrastructure notifications need anyway — an outbound mail
  path and verified addresses — is the same infrastructure that logs people in, so sub-project 3 inherits a working
  sender instead of building one.
